{"surah": 61, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "سَبَّحَ لِلَّهِ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِۖ وَهُوَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْحَكِيمُ ", "translation": "Whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth exalts Allāh,[1] and He is the Exalted in Might, the Wise.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَبَّحَ", "translit": "َḥَّbَs", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْعَزِيزُ", "translit": "ُzyِzَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْحَكِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِkَḥْlٱ", "root": "حكم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ لِمَ تَقُولُونَ مَا لَا تَفْعَلُونَ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, why do you say what you do not do?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِمَ", "translit": "َmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "تَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَt", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَفْعَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَʿعْfَt", "root": "فعل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "كَبُرَ مَقْتًا عِندَ ٱللَّهِ أَن تَقُولُوا۟ مَا لَا تَفْعَلُونَ ", "translation": "Greatly hateful in the sight of Allāh is that you say what you do not do.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَبُرَ", "translit": "َrُbَk", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَقْتًا", "translit": "āًtْqَm", "root": "مقت"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَقُولُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlwُqَt", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَفْعَلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlَʿعْfَt", "root": "فعل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يُحِبُّ ٱلَّذِينَ يُقَٰتِلُونَ فِى سَبِيلِهِۦ صَفًّا كَأَنَّهُم بُنْيَٰنٌ مَّرْصُوصٌ ", "translation": "Indeed, Allāh loves those who fight in His cause in a row as though they are a [single] structure joined firmly.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّهَ", "translit": "َhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُحِبُّ", "translit": "ُّbِḥُy", "root": "حبب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يُقَٰتِلُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlِtَٰqُy", "root": "قتل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "سَبِيلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "صَفًّا", "translit": "āًّfَṣ", "root": "صفف"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "كَأَنَّهُم", "translit": "mُhَّnَaʾَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بُنْيَٰنٌ", "translit": "ٌnَٰyْnُb", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَّرْصُوصٌ", "translit": "ٌṣwُṣْrَّm", "root": "رصص"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "وَإِذْ قَالَ مُوسَىٰ لِقَوْمِهِۦ يَٰقَوْمِ لِمَ تُؤْذُونَنِى وَقَد تَّعْلَمُونَ أَنِّى رَسُولُ ٱللَّهِ إِلَيْكُمْۖ فَلَمَّا زَاغُوٓا۟ أَزَاغَ ٱللَّهُ قُلُوبَهُمْۚ وَٱللَّهُ لَا يَهْدِى ٱلْقَوْمَ ٱلْفَٰسِقِينَ ", "translation": "And [mention, O Muḥammad], when Moses said to his people, \"O my people, why do you harm me while you certainly know that I am the messenger of Allāh to you?\" And when they deviated, Allāh caused their hearts to deviate. And Allāh does not guide the defiantly disobedient people.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِقَوْمِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِmْwَqِl", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَٰقَوْمِ", "translit": "ِmْwَqَٰy", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لِمَ", "translit": "َmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تُؤْذُونَنِى", "translit": "àِnَnwُhdْwʾُt", "root": "اذي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَقَد", "translit": "dَqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "تَّعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَّt", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَنِّى", "translit": "àِّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "رَسُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "فَلَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "زَاغُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُغāَz", "root": "زيغ"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَزَاغَ", "translit": "َغāَzَaʾ", "root": "زيغ"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "قُلُوبَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَbwُlُq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "يَهْدِى", "translit": "àِdْhَy", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱلْفَٰسِقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqِsَٰfْlٱ", "root": "فسق"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "وَإِذْ قَالَ عِيسَى ٱبْنُ مَرْيَمَ يَٰبَنِىٓ إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ إِنِّى رَسُولُ ٱللَّهِ إِلَيْكُم مُّصَدِّقًا لِّمَا بَيْنَ يَدَىَّ مِنَ ٱلتَّوْرَىٰةِ وَمُبَشِّرًۢا بِرَسُولٍ يَأْتِى مِنۢ بَعْدِى ٱسْمُهُۥٓ أَحْمَدُۖ فَلَمَّا جَآءَهُم بِٱلْبَيِّنَٰتِ قَالُوا۟ هَٰذَا سِحْرٌ مُّبِينٌ ", "translation": "And [mention] when Jesus, the son of Mary, said, \"O Children of Israel, indeed I am the messenger of Allāh to you confirming what came before me of the Torah and bringing good tidings of a messenger to come after me, whose name is Aḥmad.\"[1] But when he came to them with clear evidences, they said, \"This is obvious magic.\"[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عِيسَى", "translit": "àَsyِʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱبْنُ", "translit": "ُnْbٱ", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَرْيَمَ", "translit": "َmَyْrَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَٰبَنِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِnَbَٰy", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِءَٰٓrْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنِّى", "translit": "àِّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَسُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُم", "translit": "mُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مُّصَدِّقًا", "translit": "āًqِّdَṣُّm", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لِّمَا", "translit": "āَmِّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بَيْنَ", "translit": "َnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَدَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَdَy", "root": "يدي"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلتَّوْرَىٰةِ", "translit": "ِẗٰàَrْwَّtlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَمُبَشِّرًۢا", "translit": "āًۢrِّhsَbُmَw", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "بِرَسُولٍ", "translit": "ٍlwُsَrِb", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "يَأْتِى", "translit": "àِtْaʾَy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "بَعْدِى", "translit": "àِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "ٱسْمُهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhُmْsٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "أَحْمَدُ", "translit": "ُdَmْḥَaʾ", "root": "حمد"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "فَلَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "جَآءَهُم", "translit": "mُhَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "بِٱلْبَيِّنَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰnِّyَbْlٱِb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "قَالُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "سِحْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْḥِs", "root": "سحر"}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "مُّبِينٌ", "translit": "ٌnyِbُّm", "root": "بين"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَمَنْ أَظْلَمُ مِمَّنِ ٱفْتَرَىٰ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ ٱلْكَذِبَ وَهُوَ يُدْعَىٰٓ إِلَى ٱلْإِسْلَٰمِۚ وَٱللَّهُ لَا يَهْدِى ٱلْقَوْمَ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ ", "translation": "And who is more unjust than one who invents about Allāh untruth while he is being invited to Islām. And Allāh does not guide the wrongdoing people.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَنْ", "translit": "ْnَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَظْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْẓَaʾ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِمَّنِ", "translit": "ِnَّmِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱفْتَرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrَtْfٱ", "root": "فري"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْكَذِبَ", "translit": "َbِhdَkْlٱ", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يُدْعَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَʿعْdُy", "root": "دعو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْإِسْلَٰمِ", "translit": "ِmَٰlْsِiʾْlٱ", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَهْدِى", "translit": "àِdْhَy", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِlَّٰẓlٱ", "root": "ظلم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "يُرِيدُونَ لِيُطْفِـُٔوا۟ نُورَ ٱللَّهِ بِأَفْوَٰهِهِمْ وَٱللَّهُ مُتِمُّ نُورِهِۦ وَلَوْ كَرِهَ ٱلْكَٰفِرُونَ ", "translation": "They want to extinguish the light of Allāh with their mouths, but Allāh will perfect His light, although the disbelievers dislike it.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يُرِيدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdyِrُy", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِيُطْفِـُٔوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُٔـِfْṭُyِl", "root": "طفا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نُورَ", "translit": "َrwُn", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِأَفْوَٰهِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِhَٰwْfَaʾِb", "root": "فوه"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُتِمُّ", "translit": "ُّmِtُm", "root": "تمم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نُورِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِrwُn", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "كَرِهَ", "translit": "َhِrَk", "root": "كره"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْكَٰفِرُونَ", "translit": "َnwُrِfَٰkْlٱ", "root": "كفر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "هُوَ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَرْسَلَ رَسُولَهُۥ بِٱلْهُدَىٰ وَدِينِ ٱلْحَقِّ لِيُظْهِرَهُۥ عَلَى ٱلدِّينِ كُلِّهِۦ وَلَوْ كَرِهَ ٱلْمُشْرِكُونَ ", "translation": "It is He who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth to manifest it over all religion, although those who associate others with Allāh dislike it.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَرْسَلَ", "translit": "َlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَسُولَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlwُsَr", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلْهُدَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdُhْlٱِb", "root": "هدي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَدِينِ", "translit": "ِnyِdَw", "root": "دين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِيُظْهِرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrِhْẓُyِl", "root": "ظهر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عَلَى", "translit": "àَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلدِّينِ", "translit": "ِnyِّdlٱ", "root": "دين"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "كُلِّهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَلَوْ", "translit": "ْwَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "كَرِهَ", "translit": "َhِrَk", "root": "كره"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلْمُشْرِكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkِrْhsُmْlٱ", "root": "شرك"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ هَلْ أَدُلُّكُمْ عَلَىٰ تِجَٰرَةٍ تُنجِيكُم مِّنْ عَذَابٍ أَلِيمٍ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, shall I guide you to a transaction that will save you from a painful punishment?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هَلْ", "translit": "ْlَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَدُلُّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُّlُdَaʾ", "root": "دلل"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تِجَٰرَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَrَٰjgِt", "root": "تجر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تُنجِيكُم", "translit": "mُkyِjgnُt", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "عَذَابٍ", "translit": "ٍbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "أَلِيمٍ", "translit": "ٍmyِlَaʾ", "root": "الم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "تُؤْمِنُونَ بِٱللَّهِ وَرَسُولِهِۦ وَتُجَٰهِدُونَ فِى سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ بِأَمْوَٰلِكُمْ وَأَنفُسِكُمْۚ ذَٰلِكُمْ خَيْرٌ لَّكُمْ إِن كُنتُمْ تَعْلَمُونَ ", "translation": "[It is that] you believe in Allāh and His Messenger and strive in the cause of Allāh with your wealth and your lives. That is best for you, if you only knew.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "تُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُt", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱِb", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَرَسُولِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlwُsَrَw", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَتُجَٰهِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِhَٰjgُtَw", "root": "جهد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَبِيلِ", "translit": "ِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِأَمْوَٰلِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِlَٰwْmَaʾِb", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَأَنفُسِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِsُfnَaʾَw", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَّكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "كُنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "تَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَt", "root": "علم"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "يَغْفِرْ لَكُمْ ذُنُوبَكُمْ وَيُدْخِلْكُمْ جَنَّٰتٍ تَجْرِى مِن تَحْتِهَا ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ وَمَسَٰكِنَ طَيِّبَةً فِى جَنَّٰتِ عَدْنٍۚ ذَٰلِكَ ٱلْفَوْزُ ٱلْعَظِيمُ ", "translation": "He will forgive for you your sins and admit you to gardens beneath which rivers flow and pleasant dwellings in gardens of perpetual residence. That is the great attainment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَغْفِرْ", "translit": "ْrِfْغَy", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ذُنُوبَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَbwُnُhd", "root": "ذنب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَيُدْخِلْكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkْlِhkْdُyَw", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَجْرِى", "translit": "àِrْjgَt", "root": "جري"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَحْتِهَا", "translit": "āَhِtْḥَt", "root": "تحت"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنْهَٰرُ", "translit": "ُrَٰhْnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَسَٰكِنَ", "translit": "َnِkَٰsَmَw", "root": "سكن"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "طَيِّبَةً", "translit": "ًẗَbِّyَṭ", "root": "طيب"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "عَدْنٍ", "translit": "ٍnْdَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱلْفَوْزُ", "translit": "ُzْwَfْlٱ", "root": "فوز"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلْعَظِيمُ", "translit": "ُmyِẓَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عظم"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "وَأُخْرَىٰ تُحِبُّونَهَاۖ نَصْرٌ مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ وَفَتْحٌ قَرِيبٌۗ وَبَشِّرِ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ ", "translation": "And [you will obtain] another [favor] that you love - victory from Allāh and an imminent conquest; and give good tidings to the believers.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأُخْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْhkُaʾَw", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُحِبُّونَهَا", "translit": "āَhَnwُّbِḥُt", "root": "حبب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَصْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْṣَn", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَفَتْحٌ", "translit": "ٌḥْtَfَw", "root": "فتح"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَرِيبٌ", "translit": "ٌbyِrَq", "root": "قرب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "وَبَشِّرِ", "translit": "ِrِّhsَbَw", "root": "بشر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ", "translit": "َnyِnِmْwʾُmْlٱ", "root": "امن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ كُونُوٓا۟ أَنصَارَ ٱللَّهِ كَمَا قَالَ عِيسَى ٱبْنُ مَرْيَمَ لِلْحَوَارِيِّۦنَ مَنْ أَنصَارِىٓ إِلَى ٱللَّهِۖ قَالَ ٱلْحَوَارِيُّونَ نَحْنُ أَنصَارُ ٱللَّهِۖ فَـَٔامَنَت طَّآئِفَةٌ مِّنۢ بَنِىٓ إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ وَكَفَرَت طَّآئِفَةٌۖ فَأَيَّدْنَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ عَلَىٰ عَدُوِّهِمْ فَأَصْبَحُوا۟ ظَٰهِرِينَ ", "translation": "O you who have believed, be supporters of Allāh, as when Jesus, the son of Mary, said to the disciples, \"Who are my supporters for Allāh?\" The disciples said, \"We are supporters of Allāh.\" And a faction of the Children of Israel believed and a faction disbelieved. So We supported those who believed against their enemy, and they became dominant.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَٰٓأَيُّهَا", "translit": "āَhُّyَaʾَٰٓy", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كُونُوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُnwُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَنصَارَ", "translit": "َrāَṣnَaʾ", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَمَا", "translit": "āَmَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "عِيسَى", "translit": "àَsyِʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱبْنُ", "translit": "ُnْbٱ", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَرْيَمَ", "translit": "َmَyْrَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لِلْحَوَارِيِّۦنَ", "translit": "َnۦِّyِrāَwَḥْlِl", "root": "حور"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "مَنْ", "translit": "ْnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَنصَارِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِrāَṣnَaʾ", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِلَى", "translit": "àَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلْحَوَارِيُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّyِrāَwَḥْlٱ", "root": "حور"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "نَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَn", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَنصَارُ", "translit": "ُrāَṣnَaʾ", "root": "نصر"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "فَـَٔامَنَت", "translit": "tَnَmāَٔـَf", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "طَّآئِفَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَfِyʾٓāَّṭ", "root": "طوف"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "مِّنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "بَنِىٓ", "translit": "ٓàِnَb", "root": "بني"}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ", "translit": "َlyِءَٰٓrْsِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "وَكَفَرَت", "translit": "tَrَfَkَw", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "طَّآئِفَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَfِyʾٓāَّṭ", "root": "طوف"}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "فَأَيَّدْنَا", "translit": "āَnْdَّyَaʾَf", "root": "ايد"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "ءَامَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَmāَء", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 32, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 33, "arabic": "عَدُوِّهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِّwُdَʿع", "root": "عدو"}, {"id": 34, "arabic": "فَأَصْبَحُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُḥَbْṣَaʾَf", "root": "صبح"}, {"id": 35, "arabic": "ظَٰهِرِينَ", "translit": "َnyِrِhَٰẓ", "root": "ظهر"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"2": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "علمه وقوله تعالى لم تقولون ما لا تفعلون المراد به الوعد الكاذب وقوله عز وجل وتنسون أنفسكم إنكار من حيث إنهم نسوا أنفسهم لا من حيث إنهم أمروا غيرهم ولكن ذكر أمر الغير استدلالا به على علمهم وتأكيدا للحجة عليهم وقوله يا ابن مريم عظ نفسك الحديث هو في الحسبة بالوعظ وقد سلمنا أن وعظ الفاسق ساقط الجدوى عند من يعرف فسقه ثم قوله فاستحى مني لا يدل على تحريم وعظ الغير بل معناه استحى مني فلا تترك الأهم وتشتغل بالمهم كما يقال احفظ أباك ثم جارك وإلا فاستحي فإن قيل فليجز للكافر الذمي أن يحتسب عل", "english": "If it is said: \"Then let the unbelieving dhimmī (protected non-Muslim subject) be permitted to enjoin right upon a Muslim if he sees him committing fornication — for his saying 'Do not fornicate' is true in itself, so it is impossible that it be unlawful for him; rather, it ought to be permissible or even obligatory.\"", "book_title": "Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil", "section_title_ar": "Section 525", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book of Blame of Status-Seeking and Hypocrisy: Section 525 — The First Pillar: The One Who Enjoins Right (Al-Muḥtasib)", "section_index": 525, "anchors": "- Root: حسب (ḥ-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To count, reckon, compute; to hold an opinion or estimation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحساب: استعمال العدد\" — the act of employing number; the reckoning of deeds. The muḥtasib is one who \"reckons\" or \"takes account\" of others' actions, demanding the right and forbidding the wrong.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah (Literal): \"حسب المال\" — he counted the wealth. Majaz (Metaphorical): \"يَتَحَسَّبانِ الأَخبار\" — they seek out and ascertain news, as one places assumption in the position of knowledge. The \"ḥisbah\" is the act of account-taking, extended metaphorically to the duty of commanding right and forbidding wrong.\n- Root: قهر (q-h-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To overcome, subdue, dominate; to force against one's will.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"القهر: الغلبة والتذليل معا\" — overpowering and subjugation together. It is used of God's absolute dominance over His creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"أخذتهم قهرة: من غير رضاهم\" — he seized them by force, without their consent. Majaz: \"جبال قواهر: شوامخ\" — towering, overbearing mountains. The \"qahr\" in ḥisbah is the coercive enforcement of the right, distinct from mere verbal admonition.\n- Root: فسق (f-s-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To emerge, exit, depart; of a date, to split from its skin.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"فسق فلان: خرج عن حجر الشرع\" — he went out from the enclosure of the Sacred Law. It is more general than unbelief (kufr), applying to the one who commits to the Law's rulings then violates them, in whole or in part.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Haqiqah: \"فسق عن أمر الله: خرج\" — he departed from God's command. Majaz: \"وفسقت الرطبة عن قشرها\" — the ripe date burst from its skin. The fāsiq is one who has \"burst out\" of obedience.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its dependent, provided the intervening clause is complete in itself, as in \"إن به...\" — governing the syntactic flow of conditional and exceptive constructions in the argumentation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Enjoining Good and Forbidding Evil'", "badge": "Insight"}], "3": [{"arabic": "قال الله تعالى أتأمرون الناس بالبر وتنسون أنفسكم وقال تعالى كبر مقتا عند الله أن تقولوا ما لا تفعلون وقال تعالى في قصة شعيب وما أريد أن أخالفكم إلى ما أنهاكم عنه وقال تعالى واتقوا الله ويعلمكم الله وقال تعالى واتقوا الله واعلموا واتقوا الله واسمعوا وقال تعالى لعيسى عليه السلام يا ابن مريم عظ نفسك فإن اتعظت فعظ الناس وإلا فاستحي مني وقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم مررت ليلة أسري بي بأقوام تقرض شفاههم بمقاريض من نار فقلت من أنتم فقالوا كنا نأمر بالخير ولا نأتيه وننهى عن الشر ونأتيه (2) وقال صلى الله عليه وسلم هلاك أمتي عالم فاجر وعابد جاهل وشر الشرا", "english": "[Root: ملح]\n  • Lisan al-Arab: \"الملح: ما يصلح الطعام، وجمعه ملوح وأملاح. والمليح: الحسن. والملاحة: الحسن. وملح الرجل يملح ملاحة: حسن. وتملح: تزين.\"\n  • Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الملح: ما يحفظ الطعام ويمنع فساده، ويستعار لما يحفظ الإيمان والعلم من الفساد.\"\n  • Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"ملح الماء: إذا كان فيه ملوحة. وملح الطعام: جعل فيه الملح. وهذا على الحقيقة، ويستعار الملح لما يصلح به الأمر.\"\n  • Kitab al-Ayn: \"الملح: معروف، والمليح: الحسن من كل شيء.\"", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 52", "section_title_en": "Section 52", "section_index": 52, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "4": [{"arabic": "ويحبونه} وقال تعالى {إن الله يحب الذين يقاتلون في سبيله صفا} وقال تعالى {إن الله يحب التوابين ويحب المتطهرين} ولذلك رد سبحانه على من ادعى أنه حبيب الله فقال {قل فلم يعذبكم بذنوبكم} وقد روى أنس عن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال إذا أحب الله تعالى عبدا لم يضره ذنب والتائب من الذنب كمن لا ذنب له ثم تلا إن الله يحب التوابين (1) ومعناه أنه إذا أحبه تاب عليه قبل الموت فلم تضره الذنوب الماضية وإن كثرت كما لا يضر الكفر الماضي بعد الإسلام وقد اشترط الله تعالى للمحبة غفران الذنب فقال {قل إن كنتم تحبون الله فا", "english": "Know that the evidences of the Qur'an are manifest and numerous that God—exalted is He—loves His servant; thus, it is imperative to understand the meaning of this [love]. Let us first present the evidences of His love. God—exalted is He—said: {«يُحِبُّهُمْ وَيُحِبُّونَهُ»} [He loves them, and they love Him] (Q 5:54). And He said: {«إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الَّذِينَ يُقَاتِلُونَ فِي سَبِيلِهِ صَفًّا»} [Indeed, God loves those who fight in His cause in a row] (Q 61:4). And He said: {«إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ التَّوَّابِينَ وَيُحِبُّ الْمُتَطَهِّرِينَ»} [Indeed, God loves those who repent and loves those who purify themselves] (Q 2:222). For this reason, He—glorified is He—rebuked the one who claimed to be God's beloved, saying: {«قُلْ فَلِمَ يُعَذِّبُكُمْ بِذُنُوبِكُمْ»} [Say: Then why does He punish you for your sins?] (Q 5:18). It is narrated that Anas reported the Prophet—peace be upon him—as saying: \"When God—exalted is He—loves a servant, no sin harms him, and the one who repents from sin is like one who has no sin.\" Then he recited: {«إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ التَّوَّابِينَ»} [Indeed, God loves those who repent] (Q 2:222). Its meaning is that when He loves him, He grants him repentance before death, so past sins do not harm him, however numerous they may be—just as past disbelief does not harm one after embracing Islam. God—exalted is He—has made the forgiveness of sin a condition for [this] love, saying: {«قُلْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ تُحِبُّونَ اللَّهَ فَاتَّبِعُونِي يُحْبِبْكُمُ اللَّهُ وَيَغْفِرْ لَكُمْ ذُنُوبَكُمْ»} [Say: If you love God, then follow me; God will love you and forgive you your sins] (Q 3:31). The Messenger of God—peace be upon him—said: \"God—exalted is He—gives the world to those He loves and those He does not love, but He does not give faith except to those He loves.\" And the Messenger of God—peace be upon him—said: \"Whoever humbles himself before God, God raises him; whoever is arrogant, God lowers him; and whoever remembers God much, God loves him.\" And he—peace be upon him—said, relaying from God—exalted is He: \"The servant continues to draw near to Me with supererogatory acts until I love him; and when I love him, I become his hearing with which he hears, and his sight with which he sees\" (ḥadīth). Zayd ibn Aslam said: \"God indeed loves a servant until, from the intensity of His love for him, He says: 'Do what you wish, for I have forgiven you.'\" The expressions of love [in scripture] are beyond enumeration.", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1173", "section_title_en": "On the Reality of God's Love for His Servant and Its Interpretation", "section_index": 1173, "anchors": "- Root: ح ب ب (ḥ-b-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to love, to cherish; the root denotes inclination and attachment of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, love (maḥabbah) toward God is a real inclination of the soul toward what is agreeable; toward creation, it is metaphorical, signifying divine will, favor, and the unveiling of veils.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for creatures—an emotional inclination; metaphorical (majāz) for God—expressing volitional acts like drawing near, forgiving, and honoring.\n- Root: ق ر ب (q-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Proximity, nearness; both spatial and relational.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qurb denotes nearness in station, rank, and favor, not physical location; God's \"nearness\" to a servant is by unveiling and honor.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: spatial closeness; metaphorical: closeness in rank, virtue, or divine acceptance (e.g., \"qaruba min Allāh\" = drew near to God by obedience).\n- Root: و ل ي (w-l-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be near, to govern, to befriend; walāyah implies closeness and guardianship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Wilāyah of God for the believer is His care and guidance; the servant's wilāyah for God is loyal adherence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: adjacency; metaphorical: protective friendship and love.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and specification (bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-l-taqyīd bi-innamā)—used to delimit meanings when terms are applied to God versus creatures, ensuring no equivocation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}], "5": [{"arabic": "عز وجل فلما نظروا إلى داود عليه السلام نهضوا ليتفرقوا عنه فقال داود إني رسول الله إليكم جئتكم لأبلغكم رسالة ربكم فأقبلوا نحوه وألقوا أسماعهم نحو قوله وألقوا أبصارهم إلى الأرض فقال داود إني رسول الله إليكم يقرئكم السلام ويقول لكم ألا تسألون حاجة ألا تنادوني أسمع صوتكم وكلامكم فإنكم أحبائي وأصفيائي وأوليائي أفرح لفرحكم وأسارع إلى محبتكم وأنظر إليكم في كل ساعة نظر الوالدة الشفيقة الرفيقة قال فجرت الدموع على خدودهم فقال شيخهم سبحانك سبحانك نحن عبيدك وبنو عبيدك فاغفر لنا ما قطع قلوبنا عن ذكرك فيما مضى من أعمارنا وقال الآخر سبحانك سبح", "english": "And His saying, Glorified and Exalted is He: {نُورُهُمْ يَسْعَىٰ بَيْنَ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَبِأَيْمَانِهِمْ يَقُولُونَ رَبَّنَا أَتْمِمْ لَنَا نُورَنَا} [\"Their light will run before them and on their right hands; they will say, 'Our Lord, perfect for us our light'\"] (Q 66:8) — this is susceptible to the meaning that He bestows upon them the perfection of light whenever they had provisioned from the world the origin of light. And it is also susceptible that the intended meaning is the perfection of light in what was not illuminated in this world with an illumination that stands in need of further completion and radiance — so that this is what is meant by its perfection. And His saying, Exalted is He: {انْظُرُونَا نَقْتَبِسْ مِنْ نُورِكُمْ قِيلَ ارْجِعُوا وَرَاءَكُمْ فَالْتَمِسُوا نُورًا} [\"Look upon us so we may borrow from your light. It will be said, 'Return behind you and seek light'\"] (Q 57:13) — this indicates that the lights must have their origin provisioned in this world, then in the Hereafter they increase in radiance. But as for a light being newly generated [there without prior origin] — no. And the judgment in this matter is a venture of conjecture, perilous, and there has not been unveiled to us therein anything trustworthy. So we ask God, Exalted is He, to increase us in knowledge and right guidance, and to show us the truth as truth.", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1171", "section_title_en": "On the Ever-Replenished Delights of the Hereafter and the Yearning (Shawq) for the Divine Vision", "section_index": 1171, "anchors": "- Root: لذذ (Ladhdhah — Pleasure/Delight)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes the sweetness of taste and the experience of sensory gratification; its verbal form (ladhdha) implies direct apprehension of what is agreeable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, true pleasure (al-ladhdhah al-ʿuẓmā) is the soul's perception of what is conformable to its nature — the highest being the vision of God (ru'yat Allāh) and the bliss of proximity to Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for sensory delight; metaphorical (majāz) for intellectual and spiritual joys, where the \"taste\" is of gnosis (maʿrifah), not of the palate.\n\n- Root: شوق (Shawq — Yearning/Longing)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root signifies the soul's restless inclination toward a beloved; the \"spurring on\" of the heart toward reunion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Shawq is the movement of the heart toward its ultimate Beloved (al-maḥbūb al-aʿlā) — a yearning that persists even in bliss, for the unveiling (kashf) of ever-greater divine self-disclosure.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for human longing; metaphorical for the divine \"yearning\" attributed to God (as in the sacred reports), which is an expression of His loving acceptance and bestowal of grace, not an emotional lack.\n\n- Root: نور (Nūr — Light)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes illumination, radiance, and that by which one sees and is seen.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Twofold: sensory light (of the sun, stars) and intellectual/spiritual light (of reason, faith, and the Qur'an). The \"light of the believers\" on the Day of Resurrection is the latter — the radiance of their faith and works in the world.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical luminosity; metaphorical (majāz) for guidance, clarity, and the illuminating power of divine truth in the heart.\n\n- Root: قلب (Qalb — Heart)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root means to turn over, to flip, to transform — hence the heart is so-named for its constant fluctuation (taqallub) of states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart is the seat of perception and understanding (al-ʿaql wa al-fahm); it is the subtle divine reality (al-laṭīfah al-rabbāniyyah) that apprehends God, not the physical organ.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the physical organ; metaphorical for the spiritual faculty of cognition and volition that turns toward or away from God.\n\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Governing the precise scope of statements like \"the pleasure of what is renewed is only a distraction from the ache of yearning,\" ensuring the predicate is confined to the subject without overextension.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "زاغوا أزاغ الله قلوبهم وما الله بظلام للعبيد ولكنهم يظلمون أنفسهم ومن لم يؤهل للجولان في هذا الميدان والتطواف في متنزهات هذا البستان ربما سافر بظاهر بدنه في مدة مديدة فراسخ معدودة مغتنما بها تجارة للدنيا أو ذخيرة للآخرة فإن كان مطلبه العلم والدين أو الكفاية للاستعانة على الدين كان من سالكي سبيل الآخرة وكان له في سفره شروط وآداب إن أهملها كان من عمال الدنيا وأتباع الشيطان وإن واظب عليها لم يخل سفره عن فوائد تلحقه بعمال الآخرة ونحن نذكر آدابه وشروطه في بابين إن شاء الله تعالى", "english": "And to this journey God — exalted be He — has summoned [us] by His saying: {«سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَاتِنَا فِي الْآفَاقِ وَفِي أَنْفُسِهِمْ»} [We shall show them Our signs in the horizons and in their own selves — Fussilat 41:53], and by His saying — exalted be He: {«وَفِي الْأَرْضِ آيَاتٌ لِلْمُوقِنِينَ * وَفِي أَنْفُسِكُمْ أَفَلَا تُبْصِرُونَ»} [And in the earth are signs for those who are certain; and in your own selves — do you not then see? — Dhāriyāt 51:20-21]. And upon sitting back from this journey, censure fell by His saying — exalted be He: {«وَإِنَّكُمْ لَتَمُرُّونَ عَلَيْهِمْ مُصْبِحِينَ * وَبِاللَّيْلِ أَفَلَا تَعْقِلُونَ»} [And indeed you pass by them in the morning, and by night — do you not then comprehend? — Ṣāffāt 37:137-138], and by His saying — exalted be He: {«وَكَأَيِّنْ مِنْ آيَةٍ فِي السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ يَمُرُّونَ عَلَيْهَا وَهُمْ عَنْهَا مُعْرِضُونَ»} [And how many a sign in the heavens and the earth do they pass by, while they are from it turning away — Yūsuf 12:105]. So whoever is facilitated this journey, he never ceases in his traveling to take pleasure in a Garden whose breadth is the heavens and the earth — while he is still with his body at rest, settled in his homeland.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Travel", "section_title_ar": "Section 464", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquettes of Travel — Being the Seventh Book of the Quarter of Customs from The Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 464, "anchors": "- Root: سفر (s-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"unveiling/disclosure\" (kashf al-ghitā'); also denotes journeying across land, and a group of travelers (safar/qawm musāfirūn).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-safar: \"the removal of a covering,\" specialized to physical unveiling (e.g., removing a turban from the head). Its theological extension: the journey as a means of unveiling hidden realities — both outward horizons (āfāq) and inward selves (anfus).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): physical travel with the body, provisions for a journey (sufrah). Metaphorical (majāz): \"a face radiant with joy\" (wajh musfir); \"the morning brightening\" (asfara al-ṣubḥ) — the inner journey as illumination of the heart's countenance.\n- Root: وفي (w-f-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fullness, completion, fulfillment of a measure or covenant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-wāfī: \"that which has reached completion/perfection.\" Awfā bi-ʿahdihi: to fulfill one's covenant completely without breach. Theologically: the complete fulfillment of the servant's journey — arriving at the full measure of human perfection.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a full-weight dirham, complete measure. Metaphorical: \"to reach completion of years\" (māta wa-ant bi-wafā'); \"to come upon/arrive at\" (awfā ʿalā) — the journey's end as arrival at full term.\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit; that which is precious and valued.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nafs: the spirit (rūḥ); in God's speech \"He knows what is in your selves\" — the inner dimension of the human being, the seat of the divine secret.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a precious thing (shay' nafīs). Metaphorical: \"the soul's blood\" (nafsuhu = damuhu); the inner landscape (āfāq al-anfus) as the terrain of the inward journey.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Prepositional Interposition (al-faṣl bayna al-jār wa-al-majrūr) — governing the periodic suspension of meaning across clauses, as in the verse {سَنُرِيهِمْ آيَاتِنَا فِي الْآفَاقِ وَفِي أَنْفُسِهِمْ}, where the dual prepositional phrases (fī al-āfāq / fī anfusihim) are syntactically parallel yet semantically interposed, requiring the translator to preserve the balanced suspension of the two terrains of divine signs.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Travel'", "badge": "Insight"}], "6": [{"arabic": "حرمك واعف عمن ظلمك // ورأيت في الإنجيل قال عيسى بن مريم عليه السلام لقد قيل لكم من قبل إن السن بالسن والأنف بالأنف وأنا أقول لكم لا تقاوموا بالشر بل من ضرب خدك الأيمن فحول إليه الخد الأيسر ومن أخذ رداءك فأعطه إزارك ومن سخرك لتسير معه ميلا فسر معه ميلين وكل ذلك أمر بالصبر على الأذى فالصبر على أذى الناس من أعلى مراتب الصبر لأنه يتعاون فيه باعث الدين وباعث الشهوة والغضب جميعا", "english": "And acts of disobedience are the requisite of the impulse of caprice (bāʿith al-hawā). And the most severe kind of patience is patience in abstaining from those acts of disobedience that have become familiar through habit — for habit is a fifth nature. When habit is joined to lustful desire, two battalions from the battalions of Satan array themselves in concert against the battalion of God (jund Allāh) — may He be exalted — and the impulse of religion (bāʿith al-dīn) has no strength to suppress them. Then, if that act be one whose commission has been rendered easy (tayassara), patience in abstaining from it is heavier upon the soul — such as patience in abstaining from the sins of the tongue: backbiting (ghībah), falsehood, contentious disputation (mirāʾ), self-praise whether by insinuation (taʿrīḍ) or by explicit statement (taṣrīḥ), the varieties of jesting that wound hearts, the kinds of words by which one intends belittlement and contempt, and the mention of the dead with aspersion cast upon them, upon their sciences, their conduct, and their stations. For such mention is in its outward aspect (fī ẓāhirihi) backbiting, and in its inward aspect (fī bāṭinihi) self-praise. In it the soul has two lusts: one is the negation of the other [person], and the other is the affirmation of oneself. And by these two together there is completed for it that lordship (rubūbiyyah) which is innate in its disposition — and which is the very opposite of what it has been commanded regarding servanthood (ʿubūdiyyah). And because of the conjunction of the two lusts, and the ease of moving the tongue, and the fact that this becomes habitual in everyday converse, patience in abstaining from it becomes difficult. And it is among the greatest of the destructive enormities (al-mūbiqāt) — so much so that its disapproval and ugliness have been effaced from hearts by dint of its frequent repetition and the universality of familiarity with it. Thus you see a man who would, for example, wear silk and deem it utterly remote [of permissibility], yet he unleashes his tongue the whole day long against the reputations (aʿrāḍ) of people and does not deem that objectionable — notwithstanding what has come down in the report that backbiting is more severe than adultery (zinā). And God — may He be exalted — said: {«ودع أذاهم وتوكل على الله»} \"And leave aside their harm and put thy trust in God\" (Q. 33:48). And He said — may He be exalted: {«واصبر على ما يقولون واهجرهم هجرا جميلا»} \"And be patient over what they say, and depart from them with a gracious departure\" (Q. 73:10). And He said — may He be exalted: {«ولقد نعلم أنك يضيق صدرك بما يقولون فسبح بحمد ربك»} \"And indeed We know that thy breast is straitened by what they say; so glorify with the praise of thy Lord\" (Q. 15:97–98) — to the end of the verse. And He said — may He be exalted: {«ولتسمعن من الذين أوتوا الكتاب من قبلكم ومن الذين أشركوا أذى كثيرا وإن تصبروا وتتقوا فإن ذلك من عزم الأمور»} \"And you shall surely hear from those who were given the Scripture before you and from those who associate [others with God] much harm. And if you are patient and God-fearing, indeed that is of the resolute affairs\" (Q. 3:186) — that is, that you be patient in refraining from requital (mukāfaʾah). And for this reason God — may He be exalted — praised those who pardon others their due rights in retaliation (qiṣāṣ) and otherwise, saying — may He be exalted: {«وإن عاقبتم فعاقبوا بمثل ما عوقبتم به ولئن صبرتم لهو خير الصابرين»} \"And if you punish, then punish with the like of that wherewith you were afflicted. And if you are patient, it is surely better for the patient\" (Q. 16:126). And he — upon him be peace — said: \"Maintain the bond with him who cuts you off, and give to him who withholds from you, and pardon him who wrongs you.\" And I have seen in the Gospel that ʿĪsā ibn Maryam — upon him be peace — said: \"It was said to you of old: an eye for an eye and a nose for a nose. But I say to you: do not resist evil. Rather, whoever strikes your right cheek, turn to him PageV04P071 the left cheek; and whoever takes your cloak, give him your garment; and whoever compels you to walk with him one mile, walk with him two miles.\" And all of that is a command to be patient under harm. So patience under the harm of people is among the highest stations of patience, because in it the impulse of religion and the impulse of lustful desire and anger are all arrayed together [against the soul].", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 965", "section_title_en": "The Most Severe Forms of Patience: Abstaining from Habitual Sins and Enduring Harm", "section_index": 965, "anchors": "- Root: صبر (ṣ-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of restraint and withholding; \"ṣabara al-dābbah\" = to hold back an animal without fodder; \"ṣabartu nafsī\" = I restrained my soul.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصبر: حبس النفس على ما يقتضيه العقل والشرع، أو عما يقتضيان حبسها عنه\" — Patience is the withholding of the soul upon what reason and Law require, or from what they require it to be withheld. When exercised against calamity it is called ṣabr alone (opposed to jazaʿ); in warfare, courage (shajāʿah); in vexing misfortune, forbearance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"صبرت على ما أكره. وصبرت عما أحب\" — I bore patiently what I dislike, and I restrained myself from what I love. Metaphorical (majāz): \"هو أصبر على الضرب من الأرض\" — he is more enduring of beating than the earth.\n- Root: عادة (ʿ-w-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Recurrence and return; habitual practice that becomes second nature through repetition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Habit (ʿādah) as reiterated action that imprints upon the soul, rendering the act facile and its abandonment burdensome.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: return to a state or place. Metaphorical: the ingrained disposition that governs conduct.\n- Root: غيب (gh-y-b)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغيب: مصدر غابت الشمس وغيرها: إذا استترت عن العين\" — that which is concealed from the senses; applied to what is absent from human perception, though nothing is absent from God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"تكلم بذلك عن ظهر الغيب\" — he spoke of that behind the veil of the unseen. Metaphorical: \"أتونا في غابة\" — they came in a dense host (like tangled thickets).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between the preposition and its object for rhetorical emphasis, as in the periodic construction \"فإن ذلك في ظاهره غيبة وفي باطنه ثناء على النفس\" where the circumstantial qualifiers (ẓāhir/bāṭin) are interposed to contrast outward form with inward reality.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "عز وجل فلما نظروا إلى داود عليه السلام نهضوا ليتفرقوا عنه فقال داود إني رسول الله إليكم جئتكم لأبلغكم رسالة ربكم فأقبلوا نحوه وألقوا أسماعهم نحو قوله وألقوا أبصارهم إلى الأرض فقال داود إني رسول الله إليكم يقرئكم السلام ويقول لكم ألا تسألون حاجة ألا تنادوني أسمع صوتكم وكلامكم فإنكم أحبائي وأصفيائي وأوليائي أفرح لفرحكم وأسارع إلى محبتكم وأنظر إليكم في كل ساعة نظر الوالدة الشفيقة الرفيقة قال فجرت الدموع على خدودهم فقال شيخهم سبحانك سبحانك نحن عبيدك وبنو عبيدك فاغفر لنا ما قطع قلوبنا عن ذكرك فيما مضى من أعمارنا وقال الآخر سبحانك سبح", "english": "And His saying, Glorified and Exalted is He: {نُورُهُمْ يَسْعَىٰ بَيْنَ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَبِأَيْمَانِهِمْ يَقُولُونَ رَبَّنَا أَتْمِمْ لَنَا نُورَنَا} [\"Their light will run before them and on their right hands; they will say, 'Our Lord, perfect for us our light'\"] (Q 66:8) — this is susceptible to the meaning that He bestows upon them the perfection of light whenever they had provisioned from the world the origin of light. And it is also susceptible that the intended meaning is the perfection of light in what was not illuminated in this world with an illumination that stands in need of further completion and radiance — so that this is what is meant by its perfection. And His saying, Exalted is He: {انْظُرُونَا نَقْتَبِسْ مِنْ نُورِكُمْ قِيلَ ارْجِعُوا وَرَاءَكُمْ فَالْتَمِسُوا نُورًا} [\"Look upon us so we may borrow from your light. It will be said, 'Return behind you and seek light'\"] (Q 57:13) — this indicates that the lights must have their origin provisioned in this world, then in the Hereafter they increase in radiance. But as for a light being newly generated [there without prior origin] — no. And the judgment in this matter is a venture of conjecture, perilous, and there has not been unveiled to us therein anything trustworthy. So we ask God, Exalted is He, to increase us in knowledge and right guidance, and to show us the truth as truth.", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1171", "section_title_en": "On the Ever-Replenished Delights of the Hereafter and the Yearning (Shawq) for the Divine Vision", "section_index": 1171, "anchors": "- Root: لذذ (Ladhdhah — Pleasure/Delight)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes the sweetness of taste and the experience of sensory gratification; its verbal form (ladhdha) implies direct apprehension of what is agreeable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, true pleasure (al-ladhdhah al-ʿuẓmā) is the soul's perception of what is conformable to its nature — the highest being the vision of God (ru'yat Allāh) and the bliss of proximity to Him.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) for sensory delight; metaphorical (majāz) for intellectual and spiritual joys, where the \"taste\" is of gnosis (maʿrifah), not of the palate.\n\n- Root: شوق (Shawq — Yearning/Longing)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root signifies the soul's restless inclination toward a beloved; the \"spurring on\" of the heart toward reunion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Shawq is the movement of the heart toward its ultimate Beloved (al-maḥbūb al-aʿlā) — a yearning that persists even in bliss, for the unveiling (kashf) of ever-greater divine self-disclosure.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for human longing; metaphorical for the divine \"yearning\" attributed to God (as in the sacred reports), which is an expression of His loving acceptance and bestowal of grace, not an emotional lack.\n\n- Root: نور (Nūr — Light)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes illumination, radiance, and that by which one sees and is seen.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Twofold: sensory light (of the sun, stars) and intellectual/spiritual light (of reason, faith, and the Qur'an). The \"light of the believers\" on the Day of Resurrection is the latter — the radiance of their faith and works in the world.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for physical luminosity; metaphorical (majāz) for guidance, clarity, and the illuminating power of divine truth in the heart.\n\n- Root: قلب (Qalb — Heart)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root means to turn over, to flip, to transform — hence the heart is so-named for its constant fluctuation (taqallub) of states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The heart is the seat of perception and understanding (al-ʿaql wa al-fahm); it is the subtle divine reality (al-laṭīfah al-rabbāniyyah) that apprehends God, not the physical organ.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal for the physical organ; metaphorical for the spiritual faculty of cognition and volition that turns toward or away from God.\n\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Governing the precise scope of statements like \"the pleasure of what is renewed is only a distraction from the ache of yearning,\" ensuring the predicate is confined to the subject without overextension.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Du'a"}, {"arabic": "ينادي أين الزهاد في الدنيا زوروا عرس الزاهد في الدنيا عيسى ابن مريم وقال عيسى ابن مريم عليه السلام ويل لصاحب الدنيا كيف يموت ويتركها وما فيها وتغره ويأمنها ويثق بها وتخذله وويل للمغترين كيف أرتهم ما يكرهون وفارقهم ما يحبون وجاءهم ما يوعدون وويل لمن الدنيا همه والخطايا عمله كيف يفتضح غدا بذنبه وقيل أوحى الله تعالى إلى موسى عليه السلام يا موسى مالك ولدار الظالمين إنها ليست لك بدار أخرج منها همك وفارقها بعقلك فبئست الدار هي إلا لعامل يعمل فيها فنعمت الدار هي يا موسى إني مرصد للظالم حتى آخذ منه للمظلوم وروي أن رسول الله صلى الله عليه و", "english": "And lightning appeared one day, and he began seeking something to which he might take refuge. His eye fell upon a tent from afar, so he approached it — and behold, there was a woman within it. So he turned aside from her, and then there was a cave in a mountain. He came to it — and behold, there was a lion within it. So he placed his hand upon it and said: \"My God, Thou hast made for every thing a refuge, and Thou hast made for me no refuge.\" Then God — exalted be He — revealed to him: \"Thy refuge is in the resting place of My mercy. I shall surely wed thee on the Day of Resurrection to a hundred houris whom I created with My own hand, and I shall surely feed thee at thy wedding-feast for four thousand years — each day of which is like the age of this world — and I shall surely command a herald to proclaim: 'Where are the ascetics of the world? Come visit the wedding-feast of the ascetic of the world, Jesus son of Mary.'\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of the World", "section_title_ar": "Section 732", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Remembrance of Death: On the Merit of Renouncing the World (Section 732)", "section_index": 732, "anchors": "- Root: برق (b-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of shining, flashing, and appearing suddenly — from which \"al-barq\" (lightning) derives; also used for that which startles and alarms.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for sudden manifestation; here, the lightning serves as a natural sign prompting reflection on transience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) for lightning; metaphorical (majaz) for the fleeting illumination of worldly life that vanishes instantly.\n- Root: زهد (z-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To abstain from, to disdain, to regard as little; al-zuhd is the renunciation of something while it remains within one's power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The theological virtue of renouncing the world (al-zuhd fi al-dunya) — not the mere abandonment of wealth, but the emptying of the heart from attachment to it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to turn away from a thing with aversion; metaphorical: the soul's disengagement from worldly allurements while the body remains present.\n- Root: عرس (ʿ-r-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Wedding feast; the celebration of union; also used for the bridal chamber.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the eschatological context, the \"wedding\" of the ascetic (al-zāhid) is a divine metaphor for the honor bestowed upon the renunciant in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a human marriage celebration; metaphorical: the spiritual recompense of intimacy with the Divine.\n- Root: سخط (s-kh-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Displeasure, wrath, indignation; the opposite of ridā (satisfaction).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine displeasure (sakhat) as the cause of destruction; death by \"wrath\" (sakhtah) signifies a punishment visited upon a people for their disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger; metaphorical: the withdrawal of divine mercy and the sending of chastisement.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of separating a prepositional phrase from its governed noun when the intervening element is semantically complete, as in the narrative transitions between reported speech and action.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Condemnation of the World'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "10": [{"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}], "12": [{"arabic": "وقال تعالى {ومساكن طيبة في جنات عدن ورضوان من الله أكبر} فقد رفع الله الرضا فوق جنات عدن كما رفع ذكره فوق الصلاة حيث قال {إن الصلاة تنهي عن الفحشاء والمنكر ولذكر الله أكبر} فكما أن مشاهدة المذكور في الصلاة أكبر من الصلاة فرضوان رب الجنة أعلى من الجنة بل هو غاية مطلب سكان الجنان وفي الحديث إن الله تعالى يتجلى للمؤمنين فيقول سلوني فيقولون رضاك (1) فسؤالهم الرضا بعد النظر نهاية التفضيل وأما رضا العبد فسنذكر حقيقته وأما رضوان الله تعالى عن العبد فهو بمعنى آخر يقرب مما ذكرناه في ح", "english": "I say: As for the verses [indicating the supremacy of divine pleasure], there is the saying of the Exalted: {رَضِيَ اللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ وَرَضُوا عَنْهُ} [Allah is well-pleased with them and they are well-pleased with Him] {«God is well-pleased with them and they are well-pleased with Him»}. And the Exalted has said: {هَلْ جَزَاءُ الْإِحْسَانِ إِلَّا الْإِحْسَانُ} [Is the reward of goodness aught but goodness?] {«Is the reward of goodness aught but goodness?»}. The ultimate end of goodness is God's pleasure with His servant, and that is the reward for the servant's pleasure in God the Exalted.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1189", "section_title_en": "The Reality of Divine Pleasure (Riḍā) and the Servant's Contentment with the Decree — Section 1189", "section_index": 1189, "anchors": "- Root: رضي (raḍiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning is \"to be content, to approve, to be pleased.\" Al-Farahidi notes its usage in mutual accord and satisfaction, with the form *tarāḍin* indicating reciprocal consent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In theological usage, *riḍā* denotes the will's acquiescence to the divine decree without resistance. It is the apex of the servant's station, being the fruit of *maḥabbah* (love) and the key to perpetual vision (*nazar*).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (*ḥaqīqah*) sense is \"satisfaction of the heart.\" Metaphorical (*majāz*) extension applies to God, where *riḍwān* is not an emotional state but a volitional attribute signifying the bestowal of grace and the removal of the veil.\n- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To look, to behold, to consider.\" In the lexicon, *naẓar* implies directed vision with the eye or the intellect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Elevated to the highest beatific vision (*ru'yat Allāh*) in the afterlife, the ultimate reward and the supreme object of the inhabitants of Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical sight. Metaphorical: intellectual contemplation or the unveiled witnessing of the Divine in the Hereafter.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب التفضيل والتشبيه (The Chapter of Preference and Comparison) — Governing the syntactic structures of *afʿala* (elative) forms like *akbar* (\"greater\") to establish hierarchical supremacy between two entities, as in the verse {وَرِضْوَانٌ مِنَ اللَّهِ أَكْبَرُ}.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "14": [{"arabic": "حرمك واعف عمن ظلمك // ورأيت في الإنجيل قال عيسى بن مريم عليه السلام لقد قيل لكم من قبل إن السن بالسن والأنف بالأنف وأنا أقول لكم لا تقاوموا بالشر بل من ضرب خدك الأيمن فحول إليه الخد الأيسر ومن أخذ رداءك فأعطه إزارك ومن سخرك لتسير معه ميلا فسر معه ميلين وكل ذلك أمر بالصبر على الأذى فالصبر على أذى الناس من أعلى مراتب الصبر لأنه يتعاون فيه باعث الدين وباعث الشهوة والغضب جميعا", "english": "And acts of disobedience are the requisite of the impulse of caprice (bāʿith al-hawā). And the most severe kind of patience is patience in abstaining from those acts of disobedience that have become familiar through habit — for habit is a fifth nature. When habit is joined to lustful desire, two battalions from the battalions of Satan array themselves in concert against the battalion of God (jund Allāh) — may He be exalted — and the impulse of religion (bāʿith al-dīn) has no strength to suppress them. Then, if that act be one whose commission has been rendered easy (tayassara), patience in abstaining from it is heavier upon the soul — such as patience in abstaining from the sins of the tongue: backbiting (ghībah), falsehood, contentious disputation (mirāʾ), self-praise whether by insinuation (taʿrīḍ) or by explicit statement (taṣrīḥ), the varieties of jesting that wound hearts, the kinds of words by which one intends belittlement and contempt, and the mention of the dead with aspersion cast upon them, upon their sciences, their conduct, and their stations. For such mention is in its outward aspect (fī ẓāhirihi) backbiting, and in its inward aspect (fī bāṭinihi) self-praise. In it the soul has two lusts: one is the negation of the other [person], and the other is the affirmation of oneself. And by these two together there is completed for it that lordship (rubūbiyyah) which is innate in its disposition — and which is the very opposite of what it has been commanded regarding servanthood (ʿubūdiyyah). And because of the conjunction of the two lusts, and the ease of moving the tongue, and the fact that this becomes habitual in everyday converse, patience in abstaining from it becomes difficult. And it is among the greatest of the destructive enormities (al-mūbiqāt) — so much so that its disapproval and ugliness have been effaced from hearts by dint of its frequent repetition and the universality of familiarity with it. Thus you see a man who would, for example, wear silk and deem it utterly remote [of permissibility], yet he unleashes his tongue the whole day long against the reputations (aʿrāḍ) of people and does not deem that objectionable — notwithstanding what has come down in the report that backbiting is more severe than adultery (zinā). And God — may He be exalted — said: {«ودع أذاهم وتوكل على الله»} \"And leave aside their harm and put thy trust in God\" (Q. 33:48). And He said — may He be exalted: {«واصبر على ما يقولون واهجرهم هجرا جميلا»} \"And be patient over what they say, and depart from them with a gracious departure\" (Q. 73:10). And He said — may He be exalted: {«ولقد نعلم أنك يضيق صدرك بما يقولون فسبح بحمد ربك»} \"And indeed We know that thy breast is straitened by what they say; so glorify with the praise of thy Lord\" (Q. 15:97–98) — to the end of the verse. And He said — may He be exalted: {«ولتسمعن من الذين أوتوا الكتاب من قبلكم ومن الذين أشركوا أذى كثيرا وإن تصبروا وتتقوا فإن ذلك من عزم الأمور»} \"And you shall surely hear from those who were given the Scripture before you and from those who associate [others with God] much harm. And if you are patient and God-fearing, indeed that is of the resolute affairs\" (Q. 3:186) — that is, that you be patient in refraining from requital (mukāfaʾah). And for this reason God — may He be exalted — praised those who pardon others their due rights in retaliation (qiṣāṣ) and otherwise, saying — may He be exalted: {«وإن عاقبتم فعاقبوا بمثل ما عوقبتم به ولئن صبرتم لهو خير الصابرين»} \"And if you punish, then punish with the like of that wherewith you were afflicted. And if you are patient, it is surely better for the patient\" (Q. 16:126). And he — upon him be peace — said: \"Maintain the bond with him who cuts you off, and give to him who withholds from you, and pardon him who wrongs you.\" And I have seen in the Gospel that ʿĪsā ibn Maryam — upon him be peace — said: \"It was said to you of old: an eye for an eye and a nose for a nose. But I say to you: do not resist evil. Rather, whoever strikes your right cheek, turn to him PageV04P071 the left cheek; and whoever takes your cloak, give him your garment; and whoever compels you to walk with him one mile, walk with him two miles.\" And all of that is a command to be patient under harm. So patience under the harm of people is among the highest stations of patience, because in it the impulse of religion and the impulse of lustful desire and anger are all arrayed together [against the soul].", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 965", "section_title_en": "The Most Severe Forms of Patience: Abstaining from Habitual Sins and Enduring Harm", "section_index": 965, "anchors": "- Root: صبر (ṣ-b-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of restraint and withholding; \"ṣabara al-dābbah\" = to hold back an animal without fodder; \"ṣabartu nafsī\" = I restrained my soul.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصبر: حبس النفس على ما يقتضيه العقل والشرع، أو عما يقتضيان حبسها عنه\" — Patience is the withholding of the soul upon what reason and Law require, or from what they require it to be withheld. When exercised against calamity it is called ṣabr alone (opposed to jazaʿ); in warfare, courage (shajāʿah); in vexing misfortune, forbearance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"صبرت على ما أكره. وصبرت عما أحب\" — I bore patiently what I dislike, and I restrained myself from what I love. Metaphorical (majāz): \"هو أصبر على الضرب من الأرض\" — he is more enduring of beating than the earth.\n- Root: عادة (ʿ-w-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Recurrence and return; habitual practice that becomes second nature through repetition.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Habit (ʿādah) as reiterated action that imprints upon the soul, rendering the act facile and its abandonment burdensome.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: return to a state or place. Metaphorical: the ingrained disposition that governs conduct.\n- Root: غيب (gh-y-b)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغيب: مصدر غابت الشمس وغيرها: إذا استترت عن العين\" — that which is concealed from the senses; applied to what is absent from human perception, though nothing is absent from God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"تكلم بذلك عن ظهر الغيب\" — he spoke of that behind the veil of the unseen. Metaphorical: \"أتونا في غابة\" — they came in a dense host (like tangled thickets).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Prepositional Interposition (باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور) — governing the permissible separation between the preposition and its object for rhetorical emphasis, as in the periodic construction \"فإن ذلك في ظاهره غيبة وفي باطنه ثناء على النفس\" where the circumstantial qualifiers (ẓāhir/bāṭin) are interposed to contrast outward form with inward reality.", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Ethics"}, {"arabic": "ينادي أين الزهاد في الدنيا زوروا عرس الزاهد في الدنيا عيسى ابن مريم وقال عيسى ابن مريم عليه السلام ويل لصاحب الدنيا كيف يموت ويتركها وما فيها وتغره ويأمنها ويثق بها وتخذله وويل للمغترين كيف أرتهم ما يكرهون وفارقهم ما يحبون وجاءهم ما يوعدون وويل لمن الدنيا همه والخطايا عمله كيف يفتضح غدا بذنبه وقيل أوحى الله تعالى إلى موسى عليه السلام يا موسى مالك ولدار الظالمين إنها ليست لك بدار أخرج منها همك وفارقها بعقلك فبئست الدار هي إلا لعامل يعمل فيها فنعمت الدار هي يا موسى إني مرصد للظالم حتى آخذ منه للمظلوم وروي أن رسول الله صلى الله عليه و", "english": "And lightning appeared one day, and he began seeking something to which he might take refuge. His eye fell upon a tent from afar, so he approached it — and behold, there was a woman within it. So he turned aside from her, and then there was a cave in a mountain. He came to it — and behold, there was a lion within it. So he placed his hand upon it and said: \"My God, Thou hast made for every thing a refuge, and Thou hast made for me no refuge.\" Then God — exalted be He — revealed to him: \"Thy refuge is in the resting place of My mercy. I shall surely wed thee on the Day of Resurrection to a hundred houris whom I created with My own hand, and I shall surely feed thee at thy wedding-feast for four thousand years — each day of which is like the age of this world — and I shall surely command a herald to proclaim: 'Where are the ascetics of the world? Come visit the wedding-feast of the ascetic of the world, Jesus son of Mary.'\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of the World", "section_title_ar": "Section 732", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book on the Remembrance of Death: On the Merit of Renouncing the World (Section 732)", "section_index": 732, "anchors": "- Root: برق (b-r-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of shining, flashing, and appearing suddenly — from which \"al-barq\" (lightning) derives; also used for that which startles and alarms.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for sudden manifestation; here, the lightning serves as a natural sign prompting reflection on transience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqiqah) for lightning; metaphorical (majaz) for the fleeting illumination of worldly life that vanishes instantly.\n- Root: زهد (z-h-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To abstain from, to disdain, to regard as little; al-zuhd is the renunciation of something while it remains within one's power.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The theological virtue of renouncing the world (al-zuhd fi al-dunya) — not the mere abandonment of wealth, but the emptying of the heart from attachment to it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to turn away from a thing with aversion; metaphorical: the soul's disengagement from worldly allurements while the body remains present.\n- Root: عرس (ʿ-r-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Wedding feast; the celebration of union; also used for the bridal chamber.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the eschatological context, the \"wedding\" of the ascetic (al-zāhid) is a divine metaphor for the honor bestowed upon the renunciant in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a human marriage celebration; metaphorical: the spiritual recompense of intimacy with the Divine.\n- Root: سخط (s-kh-t)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Displeasure, wrath, indignation; the opposite of ridā (satisfaction).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine displeasure (sakhat) as the cause of destruction; death by \"wrath\" (sakhtah) signifies a punishment visited upon a people for their disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger; metaphorical: the withdrawal of divine mercy and the sending of chastisement.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of separating a prepositional phrase from its governed noun when the intervening element is semantically complete, as in the narrative transitions between reported speech and action.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Condemnation of the World'", "badge": "Afterlife"}, {"arabic": "وقد قال الله تعالى يا أيها الذين آمنوا أنفقوا من طيبات ما كسبتم ومما أخرجنا لكم من الأرض ولا تيمموا الخبيث منه تنفقون ولستم بآخذيه إلا أن تغمضوا فيه أي لا تأخذوه إلا مع كراهية وحياء وهو معنى الإغماض فلا تؤثروا به ربكم وفي الخبر سبق درهم مائة ألف درهم (3) وذلك بأن يخرجه الإنسان وهو من أحل ماله وأجوده فيصدر ذلك عن الرضا والفرح بالبذل وقد يخرج مائة ألف درهم مما يكره من ماله فيدل ذلك على أنه ليس يؤثر الله عز وجل بشيء مما يحبه وبذلك ذم الله تعالى قوما جعلوا لله ما يكرهون فقال تعال", "english": "The heart is not treated save with the compound electuary (maʿjūn) of knowledge and works. And this condition among the conditions of almsgiving runs in the same course as humility (al-khushūʿ) runs in the prayer. This is established by his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"A man has nothing of his prayer save that of which he was conscious.\" And this is like his saying — may Allah bless him and grant him peace: \"Allah does not accept the charity of the one who bestows it with reproach (al-mannān).\" And like His saying — Exalted is He: {«لا تبطلوا صدقاتكم بالمن والأذى»} (\"Do not nullify your charities by reproach and injury\"). As for the legal opinion (fatwā) of the jurist that the alms fall in their proper place and that one's liability is discharged without this condition — that is another discussion, and we have already pointed to its meaning in the Book of Prayer.", "book_title": "Duties of Brotherhood", "section_title_ar": "Section 175", "section_title_en": "The Inner Conditions of Charity — Section 175 (From The Revival of the Religious Sciences, Book of the Mysteries of Almsgiving)", "section_index": 175, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To turn, overturn, transform a thing from one state to another; to rotate, to flip\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart is so named because of its constant turning and transformation between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" (to turn a thing from its face). Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; \"قلبوا لك الأمور\" (they turned matters against you)\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Fear, dread, apprehension that strikes the heart\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; cited in {«إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم»}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a fearful man, a fearful people; \"وفي قلبه وجل\" — in his heart is dread\n- Root: عجب (ʿ-j-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Wonder, amazement; self-admiration, vanity\"\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Self-admiration (al-ʿujb) is a destructive spiritual ailment that nullifies deeds\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder at something extraordinary. Metaphorical: self-conceit regarding one's own acts of obedience\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise limitation of general categories (al-ṣifāt al-khāṣṣah within al-aṣnāf al-thamāniyyah) and the conditional structure of acceptance\n\n---\n\n##", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Duties of Brotherhood'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 400, 1230], [2, 1230, 1997], [3, 1997, 2543], [4, 2543, 3068], [5, 3068, 4796], [6, 4796, 5657], [7, 5657, 6182], [8, 6182, 7191], [9, 7191, 8010], [10, 8010, 9271], [11, 9271, 10512]], "2": [[1, 400, 2629], [2, 2629, 3861], [3, 3861, 5496], [4, 5496, 5932], [5, 5932, 7501], [6, 7501, 8068], [7, 8068, 8635], [8, 8635, 10094]], "3": [[1, 400, 1107], [2, 1107, 2309], [3, 2309, 3063], [4, 3063, 3994], [5, 3994, 4512], [6, 4512, 6350], [7, 6350, 6963], [8, 6963, 7576], [9, 7576, 9154]], "4": [[1, 400, 1405], [2, 1405, 2493], [3, 2493, 3691], [4, 3691, 5247], [5, 5247, 7429], [6, 7429, 8118], [7, 8118, 9633], [8, 9633, 10914], [9, 10914, 12718], [10, 12718, 14205], [11